  #2 0.500  conf_iclr_gupta2025sparling p.4  — SPARLING: END-TO-END SPATIAL CONCEPT LEARNING VIA EXTREMELY SPAR
  #3 0.500  DBLP_journals_fmsd_JeonQSF17 p.2  — Form Methods Syst Des (2017) 50:75–95 DOI 10.1007/s10703-017-026
  #4 0.474  DBLP_conf_sigsoft_SinghS11 p.11  — Synthesizing Data-structure Manipulations from Storyboards Risha
  #5 0.371  DBLP_conf_pldi_PolikarpovaKS16 p.14  — Program Synthesis from Polymorphic Refinement Types Extended Ver
  #6 0.366  DBLP_conf_icml_PuMSK18 p.1  — Selecting Representative Examples for Program Synthesis Yewen Pu
  #7 0.360  DBLP_journals_fmsd_FismanSS21 p.1  — Formal Methods in System Design (2021) 58:469–470 https://doi.or
  #8 0.268  DBLP_conf_nips_PuEK0S20 p.1  — Thanks for the engaging comments. We will first address two comm
  #9 0.261  DBLP_conf_nips_EllisRST18 p.5  — Learning to Infer Graphics Programs from Hand-Drawn Images Kevin
  #10 0.212  DBLP_journals_fmsd_FismanSS21 p.2  — Formal Methods in System Design (2021) 58:469–470 https://doi.or

answer:
The excerpts define program synthesis in a few related but not identical ways:

- **As constructing a program that satisfies a specification.**  
  One paper says: “**Program synthesis aims to construct a program satisfying a given specification**” [DBLP_journals_fmsd_JeonQSF17 p.2].  
  Another says synthesis is “**a special class of regression problems where rather than minimizing the error on an example dataset, one seeks an exact fit of the examples in the form of a program**” [DBLP_conf_icml_PuMSK18 p.1].

- **As a search problem over candidate programs.**  
  The same Jeon et al. excerpt describes syntax-guided synthesis as starting with “**a structural hypothesis describing the shape of possible programs, and then searches through the space of candidates until it finds a solution**” [DBLP_journals_fmsd_JeonQSF17 p.2].  
  Ellis et al. similarly frame it as a constrained optimization / search problem, saying “**The constrained optimization problem in Eq. 2 is intractable in general**” and that Sketch “**translates the synthesis problem into a constraint satisfaction problem**” [DBLP_conf_nips_EllisRST18 p.5].

- **As exact fitting of examples with a DSL plus specification.**  
  Pu et al. say: “**There are two key ingredients to a synthesis problem: a domain specific language (DSL for short) and a specification. The DSL defines a space of candidate programs which serve as the model class. The specification is commonly expressed as a set of input-output examples**” [DBLP_conf_icml_PuMSK18 p.1]. This is a more concrete characterization than the generic “satisfying a given specification” view.

Where they differ:

1. **Specification form**  
   - Jeon et al.: generic “given specification” [DBLP_journals_fmsd_JeonQSF17 p.2].  
   - Pu et al.: specifically “**a set of input-output examples**” [DBLP_conf_icml_PuMSK18 p.1].  
   So the excerpts disagree in emphasis: one is general, the other narrows the specification to examples.

2. **Problem framing**  
   - Pu et al. explicitly call synthesis “**a special class of regression problems**” [DBLP_conf_icml_PuMSK18 p.1].  
   - Jeon et al. instead frame it as “**construct[ing] a program satisfying a given specification**” [DBLP_journals_fmsd_JeonQSF17 p.2] and as search over candidate programs.  
   These are not contradictory, but they are different conceptual framings.

3. **Search vs. optimization/constraint solving**  
   - Jeon et al. emphasize “**searches through the space of candidates**” [DBLP_journals_fmsd_JeonQSF17 p.2].  
   - Ellis et al. emphasize that the problem can be turned into “**a constraint satisfaction problem**” and solved by SAT-based tools [DBLP_conf_nips_EllisRST18 p.5].  
   So the excerpts differ in how operationally they describe synthesis: candidate search vs. constraint-based optimization.

What the excerpts do **not** show is any fundamental disagreement about the core goal: all of them agree that synthesis is about finding a program that meets some specification. The main differences are in **how specific the specification is** and **whether synthesis is presented as search, regression, or constraint solving**.
