answer:
The excerpts show an evolution from program synthesis as a way to **construct programs that satisfy a specification** to a broader, more applied role in **guiding or enabling other tasks**.

In the older synthesis paper, the motivation is stated very directly: “**Program synthesis aims to construct a program satisfying a given specification**” [DBLP_journals_fmsd_JeonQSF17 p.2]. It also frames synthesis as a search procedure over candidate programs: “**syntax-guided synthesis, which starts with a structural hypothesis describing the shape of possible programs, and then searches through the space of candidates until it finds a solution**” [DBLP_journals_fmsd_JeonQSF17 p.2].

In the most recent excerpt, synthesis is described more as a tool used within another system: “**our work uses program synthesis to guide reinforcement learning**” [DBLP_journals_corr_abs-2102-11137 p.19]. The same excerpt contrasts this with related planning work and emphasizes a more practical, task-driven goal: “**our approach learns a simple straight line program that is most likely to solve the task and then replans if needed**” [DBLP_journals_corr_abs-2102-11137 p.19].

So, the change is from synthesis as a method for **directly producing correct programs from specifications** [DBLP_journals_fmsd_JeonQSF17 p.2] to synthesis as a **supporting mechanism inside larger learning/planning systems** [DBLP_journals_corr_abs-2102-11137 p.19].
